  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVATION OF THE BURMESE PEACOCK SOFTSHELL TURTLE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO ESTABLISH A COMMUNITY-BASED CONSERVATION PROGRAM TO ADDRESS THREATS TO THIS CRITICALLY ENDANGERED SPECIES FROM THE MEAT TRADE, LOCAL CONSUMPTION OF EGGS AND MEAT, FISHERIES BYCATCH, AND HABITAT DEGRADATION FROM AGRICULTURAL ENCROACHMENT OF RIVERBANKS, POLLUTION, AND GOLD MINING. THIS SPECIES IS ENDEMIC TO MYANMARS AYEYARWADDY RIVER BASIN WITH NO KNOWN NESTING AND RARE SIGHTINGS FROM BYCATCH IN INDAWGYI. ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) CONDUCTING INTERVIEWS WITH FISHER SAND COMMUNITY MEMBERS ALONG THE MAINSTREAM AYAYEYARWADDY AND SITTAUNG RIVERS TO DETERMINE RELATIVE PRESENCE OR ABSENCE OF THIS AND OTHER SOFTSHELL SPECIES AND NESTING SITES (2) CONDUCTING ASSESSMENTS OF THE COMMERCIAL TRADE AND THREATS (3) PREPARING A STATUS ASSESSMENT FROM ACTIVITIES 1 AND 2 AND DISSEMINATING TO KEY STAKEHOLDERS (4) FACILITATING A STAKEHOLDER WORKSHOP TO DEVELOP A SPECIES ACTION PLAN (5) DEVELOPING A BEHAVIOR-CHANGE STRATEGY AND COMMUNICATION PLAN FOCUSED ON FISHERS AND LOCAL COMMUNITIES (6) FACILITATING THE DESIGNATION OF A NEW RAMSAR SITE TO REDUCE THREATS.</narrative>
  </description>
